<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anabaptism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Anabaptists</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anabaptism is a radical Protestant movement that arose in Zürich in 1525 and spread through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. Anabaptists rejected infant baptism, baptising only adult believers, and generally sought to separate the church from the state; Catholics and Protestants alike persecuted them. After the violent episode of Münster in 1534 to 1535, the movement turned to pacifism, surviving in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mennonites">Mennonites</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutterites">Hutterites</a> and Amish.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anabaptism.jpg" alt="Anabaptism" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Luyken · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dirk.willems.rescue.ncs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Anabaptism traces its origins to the Radical Reformation of the 16th century. Historians classify medieval groups with a similar approach to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> as forerunners, among them the Waldensians, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a> and the Brethren of the Common Life, and the 15th-century Bohemian reformer Petr Chelčický taught most of the beliefs now held integral to it. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> it began as an offshoot of the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Ulrich Zwingli</a>. Some of his young followers thought he moved too slowly, and the division became plain at a disputation in Zürich in October 1523, when Conrad Grebel asked what should be done about the mass.</p><p>Scholars disagree about its roots. One theory holds that the movement began in Zürich and spread from there, another that it developed from several independent movements. Research has also been coloured by enemies who slandered the Anabaptists and by supporters who defended them. It was long popular to class all Anabaptists with the radicals of Münster, and those who sought to correct this error tended to over-correct and deny any link between the larger movement and its most radical elements.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Anabaptists hold that baptism is valid only when candidates freely confess their faith in Christ and ask for it, so they reject the baptism of infants. The name is an exonym meaning one who baptises again; early members refused it, saying infant baptism was not in scripture and their baptism of believers was their first true one. Early Anabaptists set out their beliefs in the Schleitheim Confession of 1527, written by Michael Sattler, who was arrested and executed soon afterwards.</p><p>Most adhere to a literal reading of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sermon-on-the-mount">Sermon on the Mount</a>, which they take to teach against hatred, violence, oaths, military action and participation in civil government. They see themselves primarily as citizens of the kingdom of God. Practices often include the love feast with feet washing, the holy kiss, and sharing possessions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Roman Catholics and Protestants alike persecuted Anabaptists with torture and execution. Under Zwingli's Protestants, Manz became the first Anabaptist martyr in 1527, and King Ferdinand declared drowning, called the third baptism, the best antidote to Anabaptism. Anabaptism was never established by any state. Many found refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, which was more tolerant and became a centre of the movement.</p><p>The Amish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutterites">Hutterites</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mennonites">Mennonites</a> descend directly from the early movement. According to the figures given, there were over 2.03 million baptised Anabaptists in 87 countries in 2025, mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. Voluntary church membership, freedom of conscience and the priesthood of all believers are among the 16th-century ideas that have influenced Western society.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anabaptism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Zürich was also the birthplace of the <b>Anabaptist</b> movement, whose first adult baptisms took place there in 1525 and whose leaders were persecuted; Felix Manz was drowned in the Limmat in 1527.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Religious life became unusually diverse after the Hussite era: Utraquists, Catholics and the Unity of the Brethren coexisted under noble protection, and in the sixteenth century <b>Anabaptists</b> known as Hutterites built communal settlements on Moravian estates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The radical reformers known as <b>Anabaptists</b> baptised only adult believers, formed congregations separate from the state and, in most of their groups, refused to swear oaths or bear arms.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dominican friar Girolamo Savonarola dominated Florence from 1494 to 1498; <b>Anabaptists</b> ruled Münster as a “New Jerusalem” until its prince-bishop retook the city in 1535; and historians still debate whether John Calvin’s Geneva, where a consistory of ministers and elders supervised morals from 1541, was a theocracy.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The Reformed Church was the public church, and only those who conformed to it could hold office, so Catholics, <b>Anabaptists</b> and Jews worshipped apart.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dutch-republic">Social Hierarchy of the Dutch Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Early in 1534 radical <b>Anabaptists</b>, many from the Netherlands, won control of the council.</blockquote><a href="/state/munster-rebellion">Münster Rebellion</a></li></ul></section>
